>
> My apologies, I thought this was the real deal. At 3-10 minutes you can run into timeout issues. Hadoop thinks a node is dead if it doesn't collect any output in that time frame. You can raise the timeout at the cost of some trade off with robusteness and you should call the status function before the timeout expires.
>
>
> Antonio
>

Thanks for the heads up about the timeout issues....will make sure that it is set at a higher level before being carried out. but my question was more related to the use of the to.dfs , from.dfs and mapreduce functions being put together for to carry out the moving window type calculation on an xts object...i.e. are there better/faster formats to use? is the structure of the mapreduce function inefficient in some way, that could cause it to be sped up? etc. I'll repeat the code here below:

# First generate 10000 days of random univariate data as an xts object
require(xts)
n <- 10000
y <- xts(rnorm(n,0.001, 0.003), order.by <http://order.by/>=(Sys.Date()-(1:n)))

# a nieve method to apply the standard deviation function across the last 10 days using mapreduce and to.dfs and from.dfs

Looking for a more efficient method of doing this within the mapreduce framework

window.length <- 10

require(rmr2)
sd.vals <- values(
from.dfs(
mapreduce(
input=scatter(
to.dfs(
c(1:(n-window.length))
)),
map=function(k,v){
keyval(k,lapply(X=v, function(x){
sd(
as.numeric(
y[c(x:(x+(window.length-1)))]
))})),
})))

sd.xts.vals <- xts(c(rep(NA,(window.length-1)),sd.vals), order.by <http://order.by/>=index(y))

note that the object y is being referred to even though it is is not being converted into an dfs format, and is only in memory in the jobtracker node...not any of the other data nodes....(would this be a problem? and if not as suggested below...how does that data get transfered to all data nodes?)

thanks for all your hard work so far btw! is looking quite promising!

